id: interface/aperture-registry-and-provisioning-v0.1
title: "Dynamic aperture registry, presentation preferences, and provisioning"
kind: interface-governance-boundary-contract
status: proposed-v0.1
version: 0.1.0
recorded: 2026-09-05
mutationAuthorized: false
externalEffectsAuthorized: false

purpose: >
  Let one canonical Yawn be experienced through multiple selectable,
  context-sensitive, permission-safe apertures without hard-coding one interface,
  duplicating private/public semantic state, diagnosing a fixed learning style,
  or letting a View widen access, authority, or truth.

product_language:
  dropdown_label: "Aperture"
  question: "How do you want to see this Yawn right now?"
  default_experience: >
    Begin with the smallest useful question-facing aperture for a sparse Yawn,
    the attention-frontier aperture for a longitudinal root Yawn, and an
    explicitly published public aperture for anonymous viewers.
  user_control: >
    The user may change aperture at any time, choose Auto, use a saved default,
    temporarily override it, or create/import another permitted aperture.

terminology_boundary:
  view_kind: >
    A versioned semantic rendering family such as orientation inquiry,
    attention frontier, relationship inspector, timeline, proof, model, public
    story, or game world.
  aperture: >
    The current scope, depth, time window, salience, and included relations of a
    View.
  aperture_preset: >
    A named user-facing binding of a view definition, aperture parameters,
    presentation profile, temporal cursor, and fallback behavior. The UI may
    call this simply an Aperture.
  presentation_profile: >
    Scoped preferences for wording, order, pacing, density, medium,
    interaction, and accessibility. This is not a claim that the user has a
    fixed biological learning style.
  provisioning: >
    Explicit rights governing which principals, participants, guests, groups,
    public audiences, Agents, and providers may access which canonical
    resources, sources, fields, relationships, Views, or effects.
  publication: >
    An authorized, versioned public or shared projection generated from data the
    publisher may disclose. Publication never changes the underlying truth or
    grants access to private source state.

runtime_equation: >
  effective rendered Yawn = canonical semantic state at event cursor
  INTERSECT viewer/resource grants
  INTERSECT source availability and egress policy
  FILTER through the selected view definition and aperture parameters
  RENDER through the effective presentation profile.

invariants:
  - "Aperture selects visibility and presentation; it does not create truth."
  - "Provisioning determines access; a View cannot grant access it does not possess."
  - "Changing aperture does not mutate canonical semantic state."
  - "Changing presentation preference does not change question or claim identity."
  - "Public and private pages are not independent copies of the Yawn."
  - "A public aperture exposes only explicitly provisioned fields and derived claims."
  - "Private source data is never copied into a public View merely because the renderer requests it."
  - "Historical replay obeys both historical visibility and the viewer's current effective rights."
  - "A user preference cannot suppress safety, authority, consent, privacy, provenance, uncertainty, or proof."
  - "An inferred preference remains a proposal until accepted at a declared scope."
  - "Custom apertures cannot bypass canonical identity, field-level access, or effect-authority gates."
  - "Aperture availability is registry-driven and permission-filtered, not a hard-coded UI enum."

canonical_reconstructability:
  rule: >
    A portable .yawn package should preserve enough canonical semantic state,
    event/revision identity, relationships, source references and hashes, View
    definitions, accepted presentation preferences, provisioning grants, and
    policy versions to reconstruct permitted projections reproducibly.
  source_body_boundary: >
    Large or sensitive raw source bodies may remain encrypted, local, external,
    expired, redacted, or deleted. The Yawn retains permitted coordinates,
    provenance, status, and tombstones rather than requiring all raw data to be
    embedded in one file.
  unavailable_rule: >
    If a required source is unavailable or withheld, the View shows the gap. It
    does not regenerate or infer the missing source as though it were present.

aperture_registry:
  purpose: >
    Define apertures as versioned data and capabilities so new representations
    can be added without adding another hard-coded route union or page-specific
    switch statement.
  required_definition_fields:
    - aperture_definition_id
    - version
    - title
    - view_kind
    - purpose
    - compatible_semantic_centers_or_yawn_modes
    - required_canonical_inputs
    - optional_inputs
    - aperture_parameter_schema
    - presentation_parameter_schema
    - interaction_operator_refs
    - accessibility_contract
    - required_capability_refs
    - public_safety_transform_or_null
    - fallback_aperture_ref
    - renderer_ref
    - renderer_integrity_or_review_status
    - owner_or_publisher_ref
    - status
  aperture_parameters:
    - scope_refs
    - relation_filters
    - depth
    - time_window_or_event_cursor
    - salience_policy
    - ordering_or_ranking_policy
    - included_epistemic_statuses
    - density
  renderer_boundary: >
    Declarative renderers are preferred. Executable third-party renderers require
    a reviewed capability boundary, sandbox, integrity identity, least-necessary
    data aperture, and no implicit effect authority.
  composition: >
    A composite aperture may embed or link other apertures while preserving each
    child View's stable IDs, source status, permissions, and operator meanings.
  portability: >
    Aperture definitions and presets may be shared or imported independently of
    private Yawn content. Importing a renderer or preset does not import the
    creator's authority, sources, private data, or accepted preferences.

initial_registry_candidates:
  note: >
    These are starter definitions, not a closed enum. Availability depends on
    context, compatible data, capability, accessibility, and rights.
  candidates:
    - auto
    - questions_first
    - attention_frontier
    - relationship_inspector
    - concept_map
    - timeline_and_replay
    - proof_and_evidence
    - decisions_and_moves
    - raw_model
    - public_story
    - agentic_yawnbot
    - nestheads_game_world

questions_first_default:
  role: >
    Questions First is the default aperture for a sparse or newly captured Yawn
    because missing orientation should be surfaced before a move is fabricated.
  not_universal: >
    It is not the mandatory View for root orientation, public publishing,
    historical replay, accessibility needs, or a user who explicitly chooses a
    different compatible aperture.

preference_resolution:
  hard_gates_outside_preference:
    - safety
    - rightful_authority
    - consent
    - privacy_and_egress
    - source_and_provenance
    - proof_integrity
    - accessibility_minimums
  ordered_precedence:
    - explicit_current_turn_choice
    - shared_link_or_published_view_binding
    - accepted_view_specific_preference
    - accepted_yawn_scope_preference
    - accepted_arena_scope_preference
    - accepted_agent_space_preference
    - accepted_principal_preference
    - context_policy_default
    - system_fallback
  transient_override: >
    A temporary aperture choice changes only the present session unless the user
    selects a scope such as this Yawn, this Arena, my private default, or my
    public default.
  auto_mode: >
    Auto may select among permitted registered apertures from task, device,
    accessibility, current lacuna, and measured local usefulness. It must show
    what it selected, allow immediate override, and never persist a fixed type
    claim without acceptance.
  inference_rule: >
    Repeated selections, corrections, task performance, context restoration,
    and explicit feedback may support a scoped aperture suggestion. Dwell time,
    clicks, or engagement alone are insufficient evidence of learning value.
  suggested_switch_copy: >
    "This may be easier to inspect as a relationship map because three active
    questions depend on the same relationship. Switch for now?"

public_private_and_shared_views:
  default_owner_state:
    visibility: private
    aperture: context_policy_default
    publication: disabled
  default_public_state:
    rule: >
      A public aperture template may exist, but no Yawn becomes publicly
      discoverable or readable until the rightful publisher explicitly grants
      and reviews publication.
  private_default_view: >
    The owner may choose a default private aperture per principal, Agent Space,
    Arena, Yawn, device, or task context.
  public_default_view: >
    A Yawn may designate one versioned public aperture and revision binding.
    Public viewers receive that projection, not the private owner default.
  viewer_specific_view: >
    Two authorized viewers may use different presentation profiles and
    apertures over the same underlying shared state. Their private annotations
    and preferences do not silently become shared semantic state.
  snapshot_modes:
    live: "rebuild from the latest permitted canonical state"
    pinned: "bind to one authorized revision/event cursor"
  sanitization_rule: >
    Public summaries must retain source and epistemic status appropriate to the
    disclosure. A sanitized derivative may omit protected details but may not
    convert uncertainty or another person's private model into public fact.

provisioning_model:
  default: deny
  subjects:
    - rightful_principal_or_owner
    - named_user_or_username
    - invited_email_guest
    - participant
    - group_or_teamspace
    - organization_or_domain
    - public_link_holder
    - anonymous_public
    - named_agent_or_service_identity
    - intelligence_provider_connection
  resource_granularity:
    - agent_space
    - yawn
    - descendant_scope
    - concept_or_symbol
    - relationship
    - field_or_claim
    - source_body
    - evidence_item
    - aperture_definition
    - aperture_instance_or_publication
    - capability
    - effect_operation
  access_levels:
    - discover
    - view
    - comment_or_respond
    - propose
    - edit_semantic_state
    - manage_attention
    - invite_or_share
    - export
    - connect_intelligence
    - equip_capability
    - authorize_effect
    - administer
  constraints:
    - expiration
    - purpose_or_use_limit
    - environment
    - device_or_network
    - provider_or_model
    - no_egress
    - no_export
    - no_onward_sharing
    - redaction_or_field_mask
    - temporal_revision_limit
    - inherited_or_explicit
  grant_receipt_requires:
    - grant_id
    - grantor_with_rightful_authority
    - subject_ref
    - resource_ref_and_scope
    - access_level
    - constraints
    - effective_at
    - expires_at_or_null
    - source_event_ref
    - status
    - revocation_or_supersession_ref
  inheritance: >
    Descendants may inherit narrower rights when explicitly configured. Nesting,
    linking, selecting an aperture, or inviting an Agent never widens rights.
  agent_boundary: >
    Granting an Agent read access does not grant effect authority. Connecting an
    intelligence provider does not grant it export, training, retention, or
    disclosure rights beyond the exact connection policy.

header_and_dropdown_contract:
  adjacent_controls:
    - temporal_control
    - aperture_control
    - provisioning_control
  recommended_shape: >
    [icon] [title + address] [Now/history] [Aperture: Questions First v]
    [Private / Shared / Public v]
  aperture_dropdown:
    sections:
      - recommended_or_auto
      - saved_for_this_yawn
      - compatible_registered_apertures
      - public_preview_if_authorized
      - create_or_import_aperture
      - manage_defaults
    each_option_shows:
      - title
      - purpose
      - current_or_default_badge
      - scope_if_saved
      - accessibility_or_capability_warning
      - reason_if_recommended
    save_actions:
      - use_once
      - set_for_this_yawn
      - set_for_this_arena
      - set_as_my_private_default
      - set_as_my_public_authoring_default
  provisioning_dropdown:
    actions:
      - keep_private
      - invite_username_or_email
      - add_group_or_team
      - create_shared_link_if_authorized
      - configure_public_publication
      - set_access_level
      - set_expiration_and_export_rules
      - inspect_effective_access
      - revoke
      - copy_permitted_link
    rule: >
      Aperture and provisioning remain separate controls because selecting how to
      see a Yawn must not accidentally change who can see it.

nestheads_aperture:
  status: proposed_view_family
  role: >
    Nestheads may be an open-world, embodied, game-like aperture over the same
    canonical Yawn state rather than a competing ontology or duplicated product
    model.
  renderer: Phaser_or_successor_game_renderer
  requirements:
    - stable canonical object IDs
    - same event cursor and replay semantics
    - same epistemic and authority laws
    - semantic mapping from game actions to explicit YAWN operators
    - accessible non-game equivalent
    - no semantic mutation from decorative movement or animation
    - permission-filtered local scene construction
  boundary: >
    Walking to, touching, feeding, fighting, equipping, or moving a game object
    is a presentation interaction until it emits a reviewable semantic operator
    with the required authority. The game world cannot make metaphorical action
    count as proof.

dynamic_detection:
  may_detect:
    - explicit_aperture_request
    - repeated_manual_switches
    - task_and_lacuna_fit
    - device_and_accessibility_context
    - correction_or_navigation_difficulty
    - measured_context_restoration_or_task_success
  may_propose:
    - temporary_switch
    - saved_scoped_preference
    - new_custom_aperture
    - lower_or_higher_density
    - different_input_or_output_medium
  may_not:
    - assign_fixed_learning_style_as_truth
    - optimize_only_for_engagement_or_session_length
    - hide uncertainty_or_hard_gates
    - persist_a_preference_without_attribution_and_scope
    - expose_an_aperture_the_viewer_cannot_access

current_codebase_pressure_test:
  findings:
    - >
      The current ViewSwitcher uses a hard-coded ViewMode union and fixed
      VIEW_OPTIONS array. This is a useful prototype but violates the desired
      registry-driven extensibility if treated as final architecture.
    - >
      The current VisibilityDropdown limits sharing to public, participants, and
      private and defaults its component input to public. The durable default
      should remain private and effective permissions should come from grants,
      not only a presentation enum.
    - >
      The current ParticipantsDropdown supports a username/email-shaped UI but
      user invitation is incomplete and linked participants receive a static
      "Can edit" presentation. It is not yet a source of enterprise-grade
      effective access.
    - >
      The current visibility resolver distinguishes only owner, participant, and
      anonymous access with full, summary-only, or hidden. It should remain a
      compatibility adapter while a granular grant model is introduced.
    - >
      The existing projection-preference schema and deterministic resolver are a
      strong substrate: they already preserve principal, scope, view kind,
      field path, operation, source event, revision, and timestamps.
  migration_rule: >
    Introduce registry and grant adapters around existing routes and components.
    Do not create a parallel canonical Yawn store or destructively replace
    visibility fields before compatibility and RLS tests pass.

acceptance_tests:
  - "The same Yawn revision rendered through two apertures preserves the same canonical IDs and epistemic meanings."
  - "Switching aperture never changes visibility, authority, or semantic state."
  - "Changing provisioning never silently changes the owner's selected aperture."
  - "A new registered aperture appears without changing a closed ViewMode enum in every consuming component."
  - "A custom aperture cannot read a field or source outside the viewer's effective grants."
  - "Questions First is selected for a sparse private Yawn unless the user or a higher-priority context rule chooses otherwise."
  - "The root Agent Space may default to Attention Frontier while a nested open inquiry defaults to Questions First."
  - "An inferred aperture preference is shown as a proposal and is not serialized as a fixed learning style."
  - "Two users can view the same shared Yawn through different accepted presentation profiles without duplicating semantic state."
  - "A public viewer receives only the explicitly published aperture and cannot enumerate private compatible apertures."
  - "A pinned public aperture remains reproducible at its authorized revision."
  - "Revoking a grant immediately removes future access and historical replay cannot bypass revocation."
  - "Invite by username/email, role assignment, expiration, export control, and revocation produce attributed grant receipts."
  - "Nestheads and a text aperture render the same Yawn identity and event cursor, and game interactions do not mutate semantics without an explicit operator receipt."
  - "Accessibility requirements can override aesthetic preference without changing semantic identity."

comparative_tests:
  conditions:
    - fixed_questions_first
    - user_selected_aperture
    - auto_contextual_aperture_with_visible_reason
    - nestheads_game_aperture
    - raw_model_aperture
  measures:
    - orientation_gain
    - correction_cost
    - navigation_success
    - context_restoration_time
    - task_or_question_completion
    - preference_reversal
    - authority_and_privacy_comprehension
    - cognitive_carrying_reduction
    - unwanted_engagement_capture
  research_boundary: >
    User preference and measured task-context usefulness may guide presentation.
    Do not claim that matching a fixed learning style improves learning without
    direct evidence.

falsifiers:
  - "The registry adds more complexity than it removes and users cannot predict what an aperture changes."
  - "Users confuse aperture selection with sharing or permission changes."
  - "Dynamic adaptation hides important information or feels manipulative."
  - "Custom Views create semantic drift or incompatible controls."
  - "Public projections leak private source or relationship data."
  - "The game aperture increases engagement without improving orientation, recall, or agency."
  - "Maintaining many aperture presets recreates the cognitive burden YAWN is intended to reduce."

links:
  - core/projection-and-aperture.yawn
  - core/inquiry-selection.yawn
  - schemas/projection-preference.v1.schema.json
  - lib/projection-preference-v1.mjs
  - interface/new-yawn-v0.1.yawn
  - interface/yawn-temporal-header-v0.1.yawn
  - interface/ranked-move-deliberation-v0.1.yawn
  - interface/yawnbot-agentic-embodiment-v0.1.yawn
  - records/2026-09-05-selectable-aperture-and-provisioning-clarification.yawn
